= 23 September 1987

Yugoslavia faces severe challenges. But much as Bismarck said that, when France sneezes, Europe catches a cold - so too, when Serbia sneezes, Yugoslavia catches lung cancer. And currently, Serbia is tearing herself apart at the seams. A bright new man, pupil of the current President of Serbia - Ivan Stambolić - has taken to the work of field politics, and come out of it a hero. Slobodan Milošević, a banker, English speaker, and most ruthless of pragmatists, has boxed his own mentor in. And he is shooting for power.

On April 27th, Milošević was sent to Kosovo as the Serbian branch of the League of Communists' representative, meant to quell and ease the nationalist fury of the Serbs in Kosovo. The Serbs, ever since Ranković, feeling under attack or otherwise dispossessed; to say nothing of their hatred for the autonomy the province enjoys due to it's Albanian majority. As the Milicija (Police, translatable as 'militia') were there a mostly federal institution, with Albanians but also other Republic members (the 'Združeni Odredi Milicije' - Combined Units of Police), they had little love for Serbian nationalism and responded harshly. Milošević, impromptu, declared famously "Niko ne sme da vas bije!" ('None may beat you!') - and from that moment on, a pupil had become a new man. An icon, for Serbia's grievances. A Ranković - without Ranković's Yugoslavism or determined communism.

Then on September 3rd, after much maneuvering, tragedy would become an opening. An Albanian JNA conscript, Aziz Kelmendi, opens fire on his sleeping comrades - killing four and injuring five. Despite the composition being multi-ethnic, the Serbian media, already largely overtaken by Slobo, runs a frenzy against the Albanians and causes near-riots. Alarmed by the hysteria, the party chief of Belgrade, Dragiša Pavlović, trying to calm tensions down - invited the media and declared that the issues could not be resolved with "Lightly promised speed". . .leading merely to his own media lynching. The true crux however, was that before a closed session of the Presidency of the Central Committee of Serbia, Stambolić went out on a limb and sent a letter of support to the Belgrade central committee, defending Pavlović. Slobo leaked this, declaring it factionalism, one of the worst charges in the Socialist system.

A meeting of the full central committee is called. Slobodan ensures that, with his allies in the media, the whole thing will be televised. And yet, one man will perhaps be more relevant, than either Slobodan or Ivan. For one man, despite Slobo's work in the SDB (security service), and Ivan's technocratic expertise, controls the SUBNOR (Yugoslavia's massive veterans organization) and JNA's massive shadow.

That man, is General Nikola Ljubičić.

And the General must make his choice.

